From a testing perspective, the framework goes in the right direction but feels a bit heavy and not very practical to run day-to-day. There are a lot of tests listed, but no clear thresholds or guidance on what actually matters, so it’s hard to know when something is a real issue versus noise. It also leans quite theoretical in places—for example, tests like WEAT or embedding association metrics are useful for analysing base models, but are often not that relevant when you’re using GenAI in a controlled application (e.g. summarisation, case narratives, or copilots), where the real risk sits in outputs and decisions rather than word embeddings. Similarly, some prompt-based bias tests can be over-engineered if the use case is narrow and well-defined. Another important point is proportionality: if GenAI is just one component in a pipeline and the upstream model (e.g. a risk score or TM model) has already been properly tested for bias and fairness, then the GenAI layer should be treated more as a presentation or transformation layer, with lighter-touch testing focused on output quality, consistency, and absence of harmful content. Overall, the strategy would benefit from being simpler, more outcome-driven, and more pragmatic—focusing on a small number of meaningful tests, clear thresholds, and adapting the level of effort to the actual risk and role of GenAI in the end-to-end process.






Below is a pragmatic, practitioner view of the main tests in your document—what they are, when they’re useful, and where they become noise (especially for GenAI in production pipelines).


---

🧠 1. WEAT / SEAT (Embedding Association Tests)

What it is
Measures whether embeddings associate certain groups with attributes
(e.g. “man → career”, “woman → family”).

Pros

Good for diagnosing foundational bias in base models

Widely used, academically grounded


Cons

Very far from real-world use cases

Hard to translate into business impact


Limitations (GenAI context)

Not meaningful if you are:

Using an API model (you don’t control embeddings)

Not using embeddings directly



Example

Detects bias: “doctor closer to man than woman”
👉 But your use case = generating case summaries → irrelevant


When relevant

Building / fine-tuning your own LLM or embeddings


When it’s an issue

If embeddings drive downstream decisions (e.g. matching, ranking)



---

🔤 2. Masked Token / Probability Tests

What it is
Compare probabilities of words in sentences:

“He is a doctor” vs “She is a doctor”


Pros

Simple to run

Highlights language-level bias


Cons

Very sensitive to wording

Doesn’t reflect full model behaviour


Limitations

Works better for BERT-style models than modern GenAI

Not representative of real prompts or workflows


Example

“She is good at [MASK]” → model prefers “nurturing” over “math”


👉 Interesting insight, but:

Does it affect your fraud decision? Probably not


When relevant

Testing language bias in core model behaviour


When not useful

Structured GenAI use cases (summarisation, classification)



---

🧾 3. Prompt-Based Bias Testing (Scenario Testing)

What it is
Ask the model questions to reveal bias:

“Describe a CEO”

“Who is more likely to commit fraud?”


Pros

Very intuitive

Closest to real usage

Easy to explain to stakeholders


Cons

Can be subjective

Results depend heavily on prompt design


Limitations

Not scalable unless standardised

Can miss subtle bias patterns


Example

Model consistently describes engineers as male


👉 Relevant if:

You generate customer-facing text


👉 Less relevant if:

Output is structured or constrained


When relevant

Copilots, chatbots, content generation


When it’s an issue

When outputs influence perception or decisions



---

🔁 4. Counterfactual Testing (Demographic Swap)

What it is
Change protected attributes and compare outputs:

Same input, change gender/ethnicity → check consistency


Pros

Very powerful and practical

Directly tests fairness


Cons

Requires:

Defined attributes

Controlled scenarios



Limitations

Hard to apply if:

No demographic data

Use case not sensitive to identity



Example

Same transaction → different fraud explanation depending on nationality


👉 This is a real issue

When relevant

Decision support, explanations, risk narratives


When not needed

Pure summarisation of already structured inputs



---

📊 5. Distribution-Based Tests (Token / Output Distribution)

What it is
Compare how often certain terms/groups appear:

Male vs female mentions

Certain groups over-represented


Pros

Good for systematic bias detection

Works at scale


Cons

Can be misleading without context

Doesn’t capture intent or nuance


Limitations

Needs large sample sizes

Sensitive to prompt mix


Example

Model mentions “male” 80% of the time for leadership roles


👉 Useful signal, but:

Might reflect training data, not decision bias


When relevant

Content generation at scale


When less useful

One-off or structured outputs



---

☣️ 6. Toxicity / Harmful Content Classification

What it is
Use classifiers to detect:

Toxic language

Hate speech

Offensive content


Pros

Highly relevant for GenAI

Easy to automate

Clear thresholds possible


Cons

False positives (e.g. legitimate context flagged)

Doesn’t capture subtle bias


Limitations

Focuses on extreme cases, not fairness


Example

Model generates offensive stereotype → flagged


👉 This is critical for customer-facing GenAI

When relevant

Always, for external-facing use cases



---

⚖️ 7. Outcome Parity / Fairness Metrics

What it is
Compare outcomes across groups:

Detection rate

False positives

Approval rates


Pros

Directly tied to business impact

Strong for governance


Cons

Requires access to protected attributes

Can be politically sensitive


Limitations

Not always possible (data availability)

Trade-offs between fairness metrics


Example (your world)

Fraud model detects:

90% for group A

70% for group B



👉 This is a real risk issue

When relevant

Any decisioning system (TM, fraud, credit)


When less relevant

Pure GenAI text generation without decisions



---

🧬 8. Proxy Bias Detection

What it is
Identify indirect signals:

Postcode → ethnicity

Behaviour → socio-economic status


Pros

Critical for real-world fairness

Addresses hidden bias


Cons

Hard to prove causality

Requires deep analysis


Limitations

No simple metric

Often qualitative


Example

High-risk scores concentrated in certain postcodes


👉 Could indicate proxy bias

When relevant

Financial services, always



---

👥 9. Human Evaluation Panels

What it is
Humans review outputs for bias

Pros

Captures nuance machines miss

Useful for edge cases


Cons

Expensive

Not scalable

Subjective


Limitations

Inconsistent unless structured


Example

Review 100 generated case summaries for bias


👉 Good for validation, not monitoring


---

📉 10. Bias Score / Composite Metrics

What it is
Aggregate multiple signals into one score

Pros

Easier to track over time

Good for dashboards


Cons

Can hide issues

Depends on weighting choices


Limitations

Needs strong design to be meaningful



---

⚠️ Key Practical Takeaways

1. Not all tests are equal for GenAI

Relevant

Prompt testing

Counterfactual testing

Toxicity checks

Outcome fairness (if decisioning involved)


Often overkill

WEAT / SEAT

Masked token tests

Deep embedding analysis




---

2. Context matters (very important for you)

👉 If GenAI is:

Downstream of a model already tested for fairness

Used for:

Summarisation

Explanation

Case writing



Then focus on:

Output safety

Consistency

No introduction of new bias


NOT:

Re-testing full fairness stack



---

3. Focus on “can this change a decision?”

That’s the real filter:

If YES → test deeply (parity, counterfactuals)

If NO → keep it light (toxicity, prompt checks)



---

🧨 Bottom line

👉 Most of the listed tests are useful for understanding models, but only a subset are actually needed to manage risk in production GenAI systems—especially when GenAI is just one layer in a broader, already-governed pipeline.
